                "To measure, monitor, and capitalize market risk across complex trading portfolios, international finance universally adopted Value-at-Risk (VaR). Formally popularized by J.P. Morgan in the mid-1990s through its iconic RiskMetrics framework, Value-at-Risk is defined as the maximum expected monetary loss that a portfolio will not exceed over a designated time horizon (T) at a specified statistical confidence level (1 - alpha). For instance, if a bank's trading book has a 1-day 99% VaR of $5,000,000, it means that under normal market conditions, there is only a 1 percent probability that the portfolio will lose more than $5,000,000 on any single trading day (or statistically, such a loss will occur only once every 100 trading days).",
