                "Historical Simulation (HS) represents the most widely utilized non-parametric approach in commercial and investment banking. The fundamental operational principle of Historical Simulation is that the future behavior of market risk factors can be modeled directly by replaying their actual, observable historical movements over a historical lookback window (typically 250, 500, or 1,000 trading days). To implement Historical Simulation: (1) The risk system collects historical daily closing prices for all market factors (interest rates, FX pairs, equity indices, credit spreads) over the lookback window; (2) The system computes historical daily relative return vectors: r_{i,t} = (S_{i,t} - S_{i,t-1}) / S_{i,t-1}; (3) Each historical return vector is applied to today's current portfolio holdings to revalue the entire portfolio and generate an empirical Profit and Loss (P&L) distribution: P&L_t = V(today's positions * (1 + r_t)) - V(today's positions); (4) The resulting P&L outcomes are sorted chronologically in ascending order from largest loss to largest gain; and (5) The Value-at-Risk at confidence level (1 - alpha) is identified directly as the corresponding percentile of the empirical loss distribution.",
